Z||000000||Philphine||07-30-2000||10:36 PM||||<BR>got a chance to play with the fiero i got this week. when i got it i was told it needed a fuel pump. i havent heard it prime so that may be so. at this point i'm just trying to get it to start for a second or even fire with quick start.<P>when i first looked at the car i noticed the spark plug wires at the cap looked like they had been twisted around. i've found out since then that they must have been trying to get it timed. but now i have no idea how to get it back. i looked at my other '84 and kind of "eyeballed" it back to position thinking if i got it close enough it might run even if rough and i could use a timing light later. i also matched up the plug wires to the other '84. didn't work. i've been turning it one way then the other trying to get results but i never get more than a half hearted backfire.<P>looked at cap and rotor and wires. looked ok if you can tell much from looking. changed plugs just because it was a cheap place to start, plus the old ones looked pretty bad. i'm trying not to get paranoid and thinking it's somthing more serious (jumped time? compression/stuck valve?<P>i'm thinking the ignition system must be putting out something because of the occasional backfire. anyone have a procedure for trying to get back to even a roughly correct time?<P> possibly a compression check to check for a stuck valve or weak cylinder? oil and radiator check don't show any visable mixing. a good trouble shooting procedure would be greatly appreciated. thanks for any help.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ggarsk||07-31-2000||12:53 AM||||One way to check the timing is to take the valve cover off. You then put #1 cylinder at top dead center. Your rotor on the dist. should be on the #1 post.You can then move the dist. accordingly if it isn't right.||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||07-31-2000||02:15 AM||||To expound on what ggarsk posted I would add the following. He said to remove the valve cover and put the #1 cyl on top dead center. I would have added; to ensure the #1 cyl is at TDC, on the compression stroke. The way to be sure is to observe the valve movement. If you're spinning the crankshaft the normal direction and you notice the ehaust valve open and close, the timing mark on the harmonic balancer pass by, and then the intake valve open, you don't want to establish your distributor timing by this series of events. Rotate the harmonic balancer another 360 degrees from the previous passing of the H.B. mark. In other words, if the valves are moving, you're not at TDC for the compression stroke, you're 360 out. This might sound elementary but I've known a few people to screw this up. If you don't want to remove the valve cover, you can guess at a 50/50. In this case, where you're unsure of the history of the vehicle, you might want to eliminate one of the independent variables and remove the valve cover. You can also remove a spark plug to ensure the timing mark on the harmonic is accurate and to ensure exact proximity of the piston in relation to the cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||07-31-2000||03:06 AM||||i forgot to add in my original post that one other reason i was looking at the other '84 is that i don't really know which cap terminal is #1. how do you determine that? i pretty much looked at the position of the wires to the electronic module and positioned it so they point the same as my other '84 then placed the spark plug wires the same way.||||reg||1
